      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=27,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd term of this geometric sequence is ${a}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $3$rd term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =27 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}(27)=18 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{3}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}(18)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{3}=12.$",
